  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 354 AM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\n- Small chances for showers and storms today\n\nLight southwest flow is in place this morning along with dew\npoints around 70F. Upstream a cold front is situated from the U.P.\nsouthwest across Wisconsin and into Iowa. We remain in the warm \nsector today with building instability. MUCAPE values even at this\nearly hour range from 2,000 j/kg in Southwest Lower Michigan to \naround 4,000 j/kg in Iowa. The front remains upstream today and \nonly slowly sags into the area tonight while losing definition. \nAloft, it is difficult to pick out much of any shortwave activity \nupstream early this morning and model data moving through the day \nshows little energy aloft either. The bottom line is a threat for \nsome isolated showers and storms is certainly there today/tonight,\nbut there is little to truly pinpoint timing or areas. Confidence\nin morphology of storms today is low as is the threat for storms \nat all. Would not be surprised to see some isolated activity \nduring the heat of the day today, but have kept the forecast dry. \nWe do have some small pops around 20 percent this evening based on\na consensus of short term models and blends. SPC has the area in \na marginal risk today, which is related to an increase in wind \nfields at 850mb and 500mb after 00z. Again, we will need a \nkicker/lifting mechanism and at this point that component is \nnebulous. \n\nHave held off on any heat headline today as index values are\nmarginal only in the far south. We could see heat index values\napproach 100 from AZO over to JXN this afternoon, but most areas\nshould hold in the 90s for maximum apparent temperatures today. \n\n- Additional shower and storm chances Wednesday through Friday\n\nThe surface front will remain in the area on Wednesday which may\nprovide a focus for showers and thunderstorms. Multiple shortwave's\nare forecast to be moving through the generally zonal flow aloft \non Wednesday as well. Strong instability will remain in place on \nWednesday making for better chances for showers and storms. SPC\nhas some of our far eastern areas towards the U.S. 27/127 corridor\nin a Slight risk for severe weather. Cannot rule this out given\nstrong winds aloft remaining in place. \n\nAdditional shower and thunderstorm activity is expected on\nThursday and Friday as well before high pressure builds in for the\nweekend. That said, quite a few ensemble members of both the GFS\nand the ECMWF indicate precipitation potential into the weekend as\nwell. Suffice it to say, forecast confidence in precipitation in\nthe coming days is not high owing to transient weaker shortwaves\naloft and a weak surface pattern most days. \n\n- Above normal temperatures through the 7 day forecast\n\nWe are expecting above normal temperatures through much of the \nperiod with the warmest days being today and Wednesday. A slow \ncooling trend is noted into early next week. It will take until\nnext Monday before we see temperatures approaching normals for\nthis time of year.",
